#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048980 :topic/difficulty 0.4269222604871827 :topic/id #uuid "d0f342d9-c70c-537f-9e04-f3b0beaf9c01" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128925 :question/id #uuid "674e7733-7a8e-5804-a867-82c74c583dcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4687" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & 1 & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 0 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 7\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & 0\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & {\\color{black}0} & 1 \\\\ \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & {\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}1} & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128926 :question/id #uuid "b6e4983b-c695-5944-9637-6d7547dae849" :question/math-academy-id "e-4688" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA= \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB= \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 7 & 10 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC= \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{4} & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129115 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128927 :question/id #uuid "97237f8e-b317-5c86-86d1-8fd3cc83e66b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4689" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A  &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{4} & 7 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}-1}} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{6} & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}2}} & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &\\longrightarrow \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{4} & 7 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{6} & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0050b2-b5bc-54a6-aeaf-94cd5d7e5dfb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129176 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128995 :question/id #uuid "7fd5c01f-c20e-5c47-b8d9-8cfbe634c14d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6166" :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA\n=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-1} & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-4} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{8} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31c3678b-4f00-54d6-bb84-c7718fd622cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Leading Entries of a Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2083 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :topic/title "Row Echelon Form"}]}
